  "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14264709/assets/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- From the graph we see that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is decreasing on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and increasing on the interval $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$ Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- The function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=-\\pi .$ Hence, statement III is false.",
